{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":24492,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"c0e4212e3a54484e934b535421a36cc1c03a08d49e9320870c4b8079b1d1c283","packTokens":9841,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"platform","tokens":215,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":110,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"sketches","tokens":9492,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":7,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"sketches"},"pointers":[],"system":"PLATFORM. This product is designed and built as a web app, and nothing else. Where the requirements ask for a mobile app, a phone app, an iOS or Android app, treat them as the same features in one responsive web app that works well on a phone's browser: never stop, park, ask or leave a requirement out because it names a mobile app, and never plan a native app, an app store step or a phone-only frame. Every page is responsive: it is laid out for a phone, a tablet and a desktop width, with touch-sized controls on the narrow one. What only a native app can do (push notifications, background location, a home-screen icon) is given its closest web form (an in-app notice or email, the browser's location prompt, an installable web app) and said so.\n\nThree engineers independently listed behaviours for the same request. Find where they DISAGREE about what the system should do (not wording differences).\nFor each disagreement: id D-1.., the span, a short topic, and the readings: which sketch (1-3), which behaviour index (0-based) in that span, and a one-line summary. Cite at least two different sketches. Empty list if they agree.","tools":[],"user":"<artifact id=\"sketches\" kind=\"sketches\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"sketch\": 1,\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat procurement PO lines as traceable source records that may map to one or multiple lots; lots and pen assignments retain the source relationships.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user synchronizes or imports purchase-order and receiving records from the procurement source. The system records vendor, costs, head counts, weights, breed, age, source, and receiving date against the imported records and establishes the lot's financial and animal-entry baseline.\",\n     \"1: A user maps a PO line to one lot or splits it across multiple lots. The system preserves the PO-line-to-lot traceability and the allocated quantities and weights for each resulting lot.\",\n     \"2: A user associates lots with pens using source pen IDs, capacities, and current animal counts. The system displays the PO → lot → pen relationships and uses lot/pen associations in downstream feeding and performance views.\",\n     \"3: After receiving data is recorded, the system sets entry weight and receiving-date baselines and calculates each lot's target weight and projected market date from its lifecycle targets and available weight/gain inputs.\",\n     \"4: If imported records are malformed, required values are missing, or source data cannot be reached, the system reports which records failed and why, and does not silently create incomplete lots.\",\n     \"5: If a repeated synchronization contains already-imported source records, the system avoids duplicating them and reflects changed source values without losing traceability to the original PO and receiving records.\",\n     \"6: If allocations exceed a PO line's received head count or weight, or a pen assignment exceeds its capacity, the system flags the conflict and does not accept the invalid allocation.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat inventory projections as estimates over the requested horizon, with the configured wastage ratio applied to projected consumption; ration comparisons use available recipe, cost, and nutrition data rather than promising biological outcomes.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user creates or updates a feed inventory item with name, category, quantity, unit, unit cost, dry-matter percentage, energy value, and storage location. The system shows current available stock and its opening, received, consumed, and closing quantities.\",\n     \"1: When stock is received, consumed, or otherwise adjusted, the system records a stock movement and updates the displayed balance and movement history.\",\n     \"2: A user sets a wastage ratio. The system applies it to estimated consumption and shows inventory projections for daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, bi-annual, and annual horizons.\",\n     \"3: A user creates or edits a ration by selecting ingredients and entering quantities or proportions, ration type, dry matter, energy, and intended animal type or weight range. The system calculates the recipe's cost per pound from ingredient quantities and unit costs and displays the recipe's nutrition and cost information.\",\n     \"4: A user compares ration mixes using their calculated costs and available nutrition/energy information; the system surfaces the relative cost and nutrition trade-offs to support cost-benefit decisions.\",\n     \"5: The system rejects or flags recipes with missing ingredients, invalid or inconsistent proportions, unavailable ingredient references, or ingredient quantities that cannot be priced, and does not present an unreliable calculated cost as valid.\",\n     \"6: If a movement would make available stock negative, the system blocks or flags it and shows the affected item and shortage rather than silently recording an impossible balance.\",\n     \"7: If there is insufficient consumption history or no configured wastage ratio, the system identifies the affected forecast as unavailable or based on an explicitly displayed default rather than presenting it as a reliable estimate.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"For pens containing multiple lots, feeding plans and activity records are pen-level but identify the lot or lot allocation they apply to; separate allocations can be recorded for multiple lots in the same pen.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user generates or enters a dated feeding plan for a pen using its animal count, daily bunk rate, selected ration, and planned delivery time. The system records planned quantity, quantity per animal, selected lot allocation, expected energy intake, estimated feed cost, and plan status.\",\n     \"1: A user updates a plan's status among Planned, In progress, Completed, or Skipped. The system displays the plan and its status for the selected pen and date.\",\n     \"2: A user records a feeding activity against a pen and lot allocation, including date, ration, planned quantity, actual delivered quantity, and status. The system calculates variance as actual quantity minus planned quantity and calculates event feed cost and energy from the ration and actual quantity.\",\n     \"3: On saving an activity, the system records ingredient consumption according to the ration mix, adjusts inventory, and rolls up feed quantity and cost to the applicable lot. The activity and resulting inventory and lot totals are visible in their histories.\",\n     \"4: The system compares planned and actual feeding and shows the variance, enabling users to identify overfeeding or underfeeding against the plan.\",\n     \"5: If the ration, pen, date, lot allocation, or quantities required for an activity are invalid or missing, the system identifies the problem and does not save a partial activity.\",\n     \"6: If the ration ingredients exceed available inventory, the system reports the shortage and does not silently decrement inventory below zero or record inconsistent activity and lot totals.\",\n     \"7: If saving the feeding event or its inventory and lot updates fails, the system reports failure and leaves the event and related balances consistent, without a duplicate consumption on retry.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-4\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat a measurement as a dated lot-level observation; the system computes weight gain from the previous chronologically valid reading when one exists.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user records a lot, measurement date, number of animals weighed, average animal weight, total lot weight, and optional notes. The system stores the reading as a new point in that lot's weight history.\",\n     \"1: When a prior reading is available, the system calculates and displays weight gain since the previous reading and makes the new reading available to performance calculations.\",\n     \"2: A user views the lot's time-series of weight readings and can see measurement dates, head counts, average and total weights, and recorded notes.\",\n     \"3: If the lot or required measurement values are missing, non-numeric, or invalid (for example, a negative weight or zero animals weighed), the system identifies the invalid fields and does not save the measurement.\",\n     \"4: If a reading has no earlier valid reading, the system stores the observation but indicates that change since the previous measurement is not yet available.\",\n     \"5: If a measurement date would make the sequence ambiguous or out of order, the system flags the issue and does not silently report a misleading gain; the user can correct the date or review the history.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-5\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Use average-weight change for per-animal ADG, and use total lot weight gain (average-weight change multiplied by the applicable represented head count) in total-weight-based FCR and cost-of-gain calculations. Project ship date as the current date plus remaining target-weight days at current ADG.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: For each lot with receiving and weight data, the system calculates days on feed, total weight gain, average daily gain, total feed consumed, feed conversion ratio, cost of gain, and projected ship date from the recorded feeding and weight events.\",\n     \"1: When a feeding event is recorded, the system refreshes feed-consumption, feed-cost, FCR, and cost-of-gain outputs that depend on those values; when a weight event is recorded, it refreshes weight gain, ADG, FCR, cost of gain, and projected sale timing.\",\n     \"2: The system compares current lot weight and gain trends with the lot's target weight and presents predictive sale-timing and feeding-adjustment insights, with the relevant trend or metric supporting each suggestion.\",\n     \"3: If a lot has no valid baseline or current weight, the system marks weight-dependent metrics and projections unavailable rather than displaying fabricated values.\",\n     \"4: If days on feed, weight gain, or ADG is zero or otherwise insufficient for a division or projection, the system marks the affected ratio or date unavailable and explains the missing prerequisite.\",\n     \"5: If a new feeding or weight event cannot be saved, the system does not display refreshed metrics as though the event had been included.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-6\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat dashboard and report filters as applying to the displayed dataset and exports; the yard summary aggregates the active lots in the selected scope.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user views an operational dashboard showing active lots and pens, animal counts, inventory status, ration usage, daily bunk rate, planned versus actual feed, feed consumed and cost, starting and current weights, gain, ADG, feed-to-gain/FCR, cost per gain, energy consumed, and best- and worst-performing lots.\",\n     \"1: A user views reports answering which pen consumed the most feed, which lot has the best FCR or highest ADG, the most-used ration, plan-versus-actual alignment, over/underfeeding, remaining inventory, and cost per weight gain.\",\n     \"2: A user opens the dedicated feed-to-gain report, filters by date range, breed, sex, or pen, and compares ADG, FCR, and cost of gain across lots. The displayed results reflect the selected filters.\",\n     \"3: A user exports the currently selected feed-to-gain report as CSV or PDF; the export contains the report data and applied filter scope.\",\n     \"4: A user views a yard-level summary of aggregate active-lot head count, average ADG, average cost of gain, and total feed cost.\",\n     \"5: If the selected filters return no records, the system shows an empty result state and does not present unrelated or unfiltered results as though they matched.\",\n     \"6: If an export cannot be produced, the system reports the failure and leaves the user able to retry or adjust the report; it does not claim a file was exported when none was created.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-7\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat the requested presentation and go-to-market analysis as demo-supporting deliverables shown alongside the responsive web-app demo, with mock records clearly identified as non-production data.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user can load sample/mock procurement, lot, pen, inventory, ration, feeding, and weight data sufficient to demonstrate the PO-to-lot-to-pen workflow and feed-to-gain calculations; the interface identifies these records as mock data.\",\n     \"1: The demo can show a user importing or mapping procurement data, planning and recording feeding, recording weights, and seeing resulting inventory, lot metrics, and dashboard/report changes.\",\n     \"2: The delivered presentation materials include the proposed solution and architectural diagrams, a demo overview, the vibe-coding super prompt and other prompts, testing and validation approach/results, challenges, production-readiness assessment, and go-to-market strategy and analysis.\",\n     \"3: If mock data cannot be loaded or a demo calculation fails, the system or demo materials identify the failure rather than presenting incomplete sample results as validated behavior.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"sketch\": 2,\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat procurement/receiving imports as source data that can be reconciled into one or more traceable lots per PO line, then assigned to pens; lifecycle targets are calculated from receiving data and configurable target assumptions.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: An authorized user imports or synchronizes PO and receiving records; the system shows imported vendor, costs, head count, weights, and source records, and links each accepted record to its source PO line.\",\n     \"1: For each PO line, a user can create or select one or more lots and assign received animals across them; the system preserves the PO-line-to-lot quantities and traceability, including one-to-many splits, and flags totals that do not reconcile with receiving quantities.\",\n     \"2: A user maps lots to fetched pens; the system shows pen ID, capacity, and current animal count, and flags assignments that exceed capacity or conflict with source counts rather than silently accepting them.\",\n     \"3: Using incoming weights and receiving dates, the system calculates entry baselines, target weights, and projected market dates for lots and shows the assumptions used; missing or invalid inputs leave the affected forecast unavailable and identify the missing data.\",\n     \"4: The system rejects or reports malformed, incomplete, duplicate, or conflicting import records, identifies affected rows, and does not create duplicate lots or silently overwrite prior mappings; a user can correct and retry rejected records.\",\n     \"5: The procurement and mapping views work as a responsive web app at phone, tablet, and desktop widths, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Inventory forecasts cover the stated daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, six-month, and annual horizons, using planned/recorded consumption and a configurable wastage ratio.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user records feed inventory items with category, quantity, unit, unit cost, dry-matter percentage, energy value, and storage location; the system displays current available quantities and item costs.\",\n     \"1: When stock is received, consumed, adjusted, or wasted, the system records a dated stock movement and updates opening stock, receipts, consumption, wastage, and closing stock; movements that would make available stock negative are blocked or clearly flagged for authorized correction.\",\n     \"2: A user sets or changes a wastage ratio; projected consumption and required procurement quantities reflect that ratio, and the displayed forecast makes the applied rate visible.\",\n     \"3: The system forecasts ingredient balances and potential shortages for daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, six-month, and annual horizons from available stock and expected demand; unavailable demand inputs are identified rather than presented as reliable forecasts.\",\n     \"4: A user creates and edits ration recipes with ingredient quantities or proportions and intended animal type/weight range; the system calculates ration cost per unit, dry matter, and energy from the ingredient data and flags invalid proportions or missing ingredient prices.\",\n     \"5: A user compares ration mixes by projected cost and nutritional/energy values to see cost-benefit tradeoffs; the system does not represent a ration as cost-optimized when required cost or nutrition data is missing.\",\n     \"6: Inventory, forecast, and recipe views are responsive web pages for phone, tablet, and desktop, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"A pen may contain animals from multiple lots; a feeding plan and activity are associated with the pen and can allocate consumption and cost to the relevant lot or lots.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user creates a dated plan for a pen by selecting a ration, animal count, bunk rate or planned quantity, and feeding time; the system calculates planned total quantity, per-animal quantity, expected energy, and estimated cost, and records a plan status.\",\n     \"1: The system shows the pen's animal count and lot composition when preparing a plan; a user can assign quantities to multiple lots, and the system flags plans whose animal or quantity allocation does not reconcile with the pen's current records.\",\n     \"2: A user records an actual feeding against a pen and date, including ration, planned and delivered quantities, and status; the system calculates actual-minus-planned variance, feed cost, and energy and retains the activity against its pen and lot allocation.\",\n     \"3: On saving a valid feeding activity, the system deducts each ration ingredient according to the recipe and actual quantity, applies configured wastage where relevant, records ingredient movements, and updates cumulative feed consumption and feed cost for affected lots.\",\n     \"4: If a ration, pen, lot allocation, or inventory balance is missing or invalid, the system explains what must be corrected and does not silently record a completed activity with incorrect stock or cost; authorized users can correct or void an erroneous activity with corresponding movement and rollup adjustments.\",\n     \"5: Users can mark a planned feeding in progress, completed, or skipped; skipped feedings do not consume inventory, and the system preserves the reason or status for operational review.\",\n     \"6: Planning and feeding-entry views are responsive web pages for phone, tablet, and desktop, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-4\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Weight readings are lot-level time-series observations; total lot weight and gain are derived from the recorded average weight and number weighed where those values are available.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user records a lot, measurement date, number weighed, average animal weight, and optional notes; the system saves the reading in the lot's dated history and calculates total measured weight and change from the prior reading when possible.\",\n     \"1: The lot history displays measurements chronologically and exposes entry weight, current weight, and changes over time; adding a later measurement updates the displayed latest reading without deleting earlier readings.\",\n     \"2: The system flags invalid dates, nonpositive weights or counts, and readings that conflict with prior data for review; if no prior reading exists, it records the baseline and does not invent a gain value.\",\n     \"3: Weight-entry and history views are responsive web pages for phone, tablet, and desktop, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-5\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Use the supplied formulas: FCR is feed consumed divided by weight gained, and feed-to-gain ratio refers to that same efficiency measure; recompute lot metrics when feeding or weight events change.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: For each lot, the system displays days on feed, total weight gain, average daily gain, total feed consumed, FCR, feed cost per pound of gain, and projected ship date from receiving, feeding, and weight records.\",\n     \"1: After a feeding or weight event is saved or corrected, the system refreshes affected lot totals and derived metrics so dashboards and reports reflect the new data.\",\n     \"2: When dates, entry/current weights, feed totals, or gain are insufficient—or gain is zero or negative—the system marks dependent metrics unavailable or not meaningful instead of showing misleading divisions or projections.\",\n     \"3: The system uses current gain trends and target weights to show projected sale readiness/date and actionable feeding or sale-timing suggestions; it indicates when data is insufficient to make a dependable suggestion.\",\n     \"4: Users can inspect the source readings and feeding totals behind a lot metric or prediction, so a changed result can be related to the recorded events.\",\n     \"5: Performance and insight views are responsive web pages for phone, tablet, and desktop, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-6\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Reports support the listed lot/pen performance questions and filters; exports reflect the currently selected report filters and date range.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: The operational dashboard shows active lots and pens, animal counts, inventory status, ration usage, daily bunk rates, planned versus actual feed, feed consumed and cost, starting/current weight, gain, ADG, feed-to-gain/FCR, cost of gain, energy consumed, and best/worst performing lots.\",\n     \"1: A user filters dashboards and reports by date range and available lot, pen, breed, or sex attributes; the system updates the displayed rows and aggregates to match the selected filters and indicates when no records match.\",\n     \"2: Reports answer which pen consumed most, which lots lead in FCR or ADG, which ration is used most, plan-versus-actual alignment, over/underfeeding, remaining inventory, and cost per unit of gain.\",\n     \"3: A user exports the selected feed-to-gain report as CSV or PDF; exported values and filters correspond to the on-screen selection, and export failures are reported without implying a file was created.\",\n     \"4: The yard summary aggregates active-lot head count, average ADG, average cost of gain, and total feed cost across the yard; it identifies unavailable aggregates when source values are missing.\",\n     \"5: Users without access to operational or financial data cannot view or export those values; the system reports denied access without exposing the restricted data.\",\n     \"6: Dashboards and reports are responsive web pages for phone, tablet, and desktop, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-7\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat this as a request for a runnable web-app demo populated with clearly identified synthetic data, plus presentation materials that explain the solution and its readiness—not a request to deploy a native app.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A user can load a clearly labeled mock dataset containing representative procurement, lots, pens, inventory, rations, feeding, and weight records; the resulting views demonstrate the end-to-end workflows and calculated metrics.\",\n     \"1: The system prevents mock records from being mistaken for live source data and lets a user reset or reload the demo dataset without silently mixing it with live records.\",\n     \"2: The demo/presentation materials describe the proposed solution and architecture, demonstrate the app, summarize prompts used, testing and validation, challenges, production readiness, and go-to-market strategy and analysis.\",\n     \"3: If demo data or an external source is unavailable or fails to load, the system reports the failure and does not present empty or partial results as a successful demo.\",\n     \"4: The demo is a responsive web app that works at phone, tablet, and desktop widths; it does not require a native app or app-store installation.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"sketch\": 3,\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat procurement PO and receiving data as importable external records, and allow one PO line to map to one or multiple lots and pens while preserving traceability.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: An authorized user imports PO and receiving records containing vendor, costs, head count, weights, breed, age, and source; successfully validated records are saved and linked to their source PO/receiving records.\",\n     \"1: The system validates required fields and compatible quantities and weights, reports invalid or incomplete rows, and does not silently create incomplete mappings. Re-imported records are identified so they do not create unintended duplicates.\",\n     \"2: A user maps a PO line to one or more lots and links lots to pens; the system retains the PO-line-to-lot-to-pen relationships and displays source head counts, weights, costs, pen capacity, and current animal counts for reconciliation.\",\n     \"3: The system flags mappings whose head counts do not reconcile with received quantities or pen capacity, and prevents or clearly identifies invalid assignments rather than silently accepting them.\",\n     \"4: For a lot with receiving weight and a target weight, the system records a projected target weight and market/ship date using the configured gain assumption or available lot performance trend; the projection is recalculated when relevant receiving, target, or performance data changes.\",\n     \"5: If required weight, target, receiving date, or gain information is missing or unusable, the system indicates that a lifecycle projection is unavailable instead of displaying a misleading date.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat inventory forecasts as projections from recorded stock movements and planned/observed consumption, with a configurable wastage ratio applied to estimated consumption.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: An authorized user creates and updates feed inventory items with feed type/category, quantity, unit, unit cost, dry-matter percentage, energy value, and storage location; the system displays the resulting available quantity and value.\",\n     \"1: The system records opening stock, receipts, consumption, and closing stock as traceable stock movements; it updates the item balance when a movement is recorded and displays the movement history.\",\n     \"2: A user configures a wastage ratio; consumption estimates and inventory projections include the configured wastage, and changing the ratio updates affected projections.\",\n     \"3: The system presents inventory forecasts for daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, half-yearly, and annual horizons using current stock, receipts, planned use, and wastage assumptions.\",\n     \"4: A user creates or edits a ration by selecting ingredients and entering quantities or proportions; the system calculates the ingredient mix, ration cost per unit, dry-matter and energy values where inputs permit, and makes the ration available for feeding plans.\",\n     \"5: The system flags invalid mixes, missing ingredient costs or nutritional values, incompatible units, and insufficient stock; it does not present an incomplete cost or nutrition calculation as a verified value.\",\n     \"6: When projected or recorded demand exceeds available inventory, the system flags the shortfall and the affected items and periods; it does not show a negative balance as available stock.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat a pen as the execution unit for plans and feeding logs, while allowing a pen to contain animals from multiple lots and attributing consumption and cost to the recorded lot allocation.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: An authorized user creates a dated feeding plan for a pen by selecting a ration, animal count, quantity or daily bunk rate, and planned feeding time; the system calculates planned quantity, per-animal quantity, expected energy, and planned cost where inputs are available.\",\n     \"1: The user can update plan status to Planned, In progress, Completed, or Skipped, and the system displays the plan and its status against the pen and date.\",\n     \"2: The user records an actual feeding event with date, pen, lot allocation, ration, planned quantity, and actual delivered quantity; the system calculates actual-minus-planned variance, feed cost, energy, and ingredient quantities consumed.\",\n     \"3: On a valid saved feeding event, the system records the event, decrements inventory according to ration proportions and configured wastage, and updates cumulative feed consumption and feed cost for the attributed lot or lots.\",\n     \"4: If the ration, pen, lot allocation, quantity, or inventory is invalid or insufficient, the system reports the issue and does not silently record inconsistent inventory or lot totals.\",\n     \"5: If the pen contains animals from multiple lots, the user must provide an allocation sufficient to attribute feeding; the system flags missing or non-reconciling allocations rather than assigning all consumption to an arbitrary lot.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-4\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat each submitted lot weight measurement as a dated historical observation, not as an edit that replaces prior readings.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: An authorized user records a lot, measurement date, number of animals weighed, average animal weight, total lot weight, and optional notes; the system saves the reading and displays it in the lot's chronological weight history.\",\n     \"1: The system calculates or validates total weight from average weight and number weighed, and calculates weight gain since the prior applicable reading when prior data exists.\",\n     \"2: The system rejects or flags missing, non-positive, or inconsistent measurement values and dates that would make the history misleading; the prior history remains available.\",\n     \"3: When there is no earlier reading, the system records the measurement as the initial reading and indicates that change since a prior measurement is unavailable.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-5\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Use the stated feed-to-gain formulas at lot level; make predictive suggestions from recorded trends, and show a metric as unavailable when its inputs make the calculation undefined.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: The system calculates lot days on feed from receiving date, total weight gain from current versus entry average weight, ADG from gain divided by days on feed, total feed consumed from feeding events, FCR from feed consumed divided by weight gain, and cost of gain from feed cost divided by weight gain.\",\n     \"1: The system projects ship date from current weight, target weight, and ADG, and presents feeding or sale-timing suggestions based on current recorded weight-gain and feeding trends.\",\n     \"2: Recording a feeding event updates feed totals, costs, FCR, and cost of gain; recording a weight event updates current weight, gain, ADG, FCR, cost of gain, projected ship date, and related insights without requiring manual recalculation.\",\n     \"3: If days on feed, weight gain, ADG, target weight, or relevant consumption data is zero, missing, or otherwise unsuitable for a formula, the system shows the affected metric or projection as unavailable and does not divide by zero or imply a valid forecast.\",\n     \"4: The system identifies the measurement and feeding data used for displayed lot metrics so users can see when a metric reflects incomplete or stale source data.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-6\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat dashboard and report data as filterable operational summaries across lots and pens, with exports reflecting the selected report and filters.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: The dashboard displays active lots and pens, animal counts, feed inventory, ration usage, daily bunk rates, planned versus actual feeding, feed consumption and cost, starting and current weights, gain, ADG, feed-to-gain/FCR, cost of gain, energy consumed, and best- and worst-performing lots where data permits.\",\n     \"1: Users can open operational reports answering which pen consumed most feed, which lot has best FCR or highest ADG, most-used ration, plan adherence, over/underfeeding, remaining inventory, and feed cost per unit of gain.\",\n     \"2: Users can filter the Feed-to-Gain report by date range, breed, sex, and pen and compare ADG, FCR, and cost of gain across lots; displayed results change to match the selected filters.\",\n     \